{
"slug": "artists-dominica.html", "fr_slug": "artistes-dominique.html", "cat": "Bouyon artists", "nav": "Artists from Dominica",
"title": "Bouyon artists from Dominica: the founders and the new wave",
"desc": "Bouyon artists from Dominica: from WCK, who created bouyon in 1988, to Asa Bantan, Reo, Shelly and BONNA, the full roster of the source island.",
"h1": "Bouyon artists from Dominica: the source island roster",
"intro": "Dominica is not one chapter of bouyon among others: it is the source island, where the genre was born and where its founders, its bosses and its new wave still come from. This page gathers the Dominican roster followed by Bouyon Shattaz, from the creators of 1988 to the names billed at the 2026 World Creole Music Festival, the WCMF, in Roseau. Every artist has a full profile on this site. To understand where the sound comes from, start with our <a href=\"/en/history.html\">history of bouyon</a> page, then come back here: a lineage, not a simple list.",
"sections": [
("The founders: WCK creates bouyon in 1988", [
"Every Dominican roster starts with <a href=\"/en/wck.html\">WCK</a>. The band created bouyon in 1988 in Roseau, the capital of Dominica. That founding act still matters today, because everything that carries the name bouyon, in Guadeloupe, in Martinique, in France or in the diaspora, points back to this Dominican invention. WCK is therefore not just an old name to cite out of respect: it is the starting point of the genre, the proof that bouyon has one island, one town and one precise birth date.",
"Placing WCK at the top of this page also sets the reading rule for the whole site. When a sound travels, credits get lost easily. Here they are written in black and white: bouyon was created in Dominica in 1988, and the artists on this page inherit that work, transform it or defend it. The generations that follow, from the jump up bosses to today's producers, are all measured against that heritage, whether they claim it in interviews or simply on stage, in front of the Roseau crowd.",
]),
("The cadence-lypso legacy: Midnight Groovers", [
"Even before bouyon, Dominica already had a great band tradition, and <a href=\"/en/midnight-groovers.html\">Midnight Groovers</a> is its legendary name. Formed in Grand Bay in the late 1960s, this cadence-lypso band is led by Phillip \"Chubby\" Mark and counts more than 39 albums. A catalogue like that says something essential about the island: live band music, rooted in a village and a language, was an institution there long before streaming platforms.",
"Midnight Groovers belongs in this roster because bouyon was not born from nothing. It grows on ground prepared by cadence-lypso, by village fetes and by musicians who recorded album after album for their public. Understanding that depth helps explain why Dominica defends its invention with such conviction: behind bouyon there are decades of Dominican music, and Grand Bay, the village of Midnight Groovers, remains one of its beating hearts.",
]),
("The boss: Asa Bantan, from Grand Bay", [
"<a href=\"/en/asa-bantan.html\">Asa Bantan</a> comes from Grand Bay, the same village as Midnight Groovers, and he is widely called the King of Bouyon, as well as Bouyon Boss. Those are attributed titles, the ones the public and the scene give him, and they describe his place in the genre: the boss, the reference artist the others have to face on stage and in the playlists. His name comes up whenever Dominican bouyon is discussed in the present tense, not only in the past.",
"Asa Bantan represents the continuity between the invention and the current scene: a carnival presence, a repertoire the public knows by heart and a natural authority live. His profile is the doorway into Dominican bouyon in its most direct form, jump up.",
]),
("The titled generation: Reo and Ridge", [
"<a href=\"/en/reo.html\">Reo</a> is billed as the Prince of Bouyon: again, that is an attributed title, the one the scene presents him under, and it suits an artist placed in the direct line of the bosses. Reo is a 2026 Caribbean Music Awards nominee, a recognition that reaches beyond the island. In 2026 he also carries one of the most visible bridges in current bouyon, \"Joli BB\" with Martinican singer Maurane Voyer, a record that links Roseau to Fort-de-France.",
"<a href=\"/en/ridge.html\">Ridge</a>, known as Mr Ridge, is the other Dominican name nominated at the 2026 Caribbean Music Awards. A keyboardist and producer, he is a reminder that bouyon is not only about singers: the musicians who build the sound, the arrangements and the riddims hold the genre up as much as the voices do. Ridge is also billed on Night 1 of WCMF 2026. His profile completes Reo's: on one side the voice and the prince title, on the other the keyboard, the production and the studio work.",
]),
("The live band engine: Signal Band and TK International", [
"<a href=\"/en/signal-band.html\">Signal Band</a> is the band behind two trending tracks by Shelly, the Dominican singer presented further down this page. That role shows the importance of bands in today's bouyon: a solid band turns a good song into a track that runs everywhere, because it knows how to play it loud, fast and long, the way the carnival and fete public demands. Signal Band is therefore worth following as an engine of the scene, not as a simple backing act.",
"<a href=\"/en/tk-international.html\">TK International</a>, the Triple Kay Band, belongs to the same culture of great Dominican bands and is billed on Night 1 of WCMF 2026. The presence of these groups at the festival shows that, in Dominica, bouyon remains band music and stage music, inherited from cadence-lypso and built for live performance. When you prepare your festival, keep these two names in mind: they are the ones who carry the island's sound when the lights go up at Windsor Park.",
]),
("The voices and the solos: Shelly and Colton T", [
"<a href=\"/en/shelly.html\">Shelly</a> is a Dominican singer whose track \"Bye to de Old Me (Remix)\" marks her path, with Signal Band behind two of her trending tracks. Her place in this roster is that of an island voice establishing herself in today's bouyon, with songs that speak of leaving, changing and standing up for yourself. For readers looking for the women of contemporary Dominican bouyon, her profile is essential reading, alongside the other voices on this site.",
"<a href=\"/en/colton-t.html\">Colton T</a> represents the solo artist side of the roster. He is billed on Night 1 of WCMF 2026, alongside the bands and names already cited. That place on the bill of the home festival, in Roseau, works as a confirmation: the Dominican scene trusts its solo artists to open the biggest Creole stage on the island. Colton T thus completes a picture where historic bands, bosses, a producer and solo voices coexist, all branches of the same tree.",
]),
("Street and carnival names: Pudaz and Gwada G", [
"<a href=\"/en/pudaz.html\">Pudaz</a>, known as Dirty Dawg Pudaz, carries the track \"Shake Something\", a title that says everything about his function at a party: make people move. Dominican bouyon always keeps a place for these pure-energy artists, whose songs are dance orders as much as tracks. Pudaz embodies that direct, festive branch of the roster, the one that links playlists to carnival streets and to the parties where the genre is tested for real, in front of a public that does not forgive a weak track.",
"<a href=\"/en/gwada-g.html\">Gwada G</a> needs one clarification, and it matters: despite his name, which points to Guadeloupe, Gwada G is a Dominican artist, a homegrown talent confirmed in the official Discover Dominica Authority release. His case is a reminder of why this site verifies every origin instead of guessing it from a stage name. In a genre that travels between islands with close names, a stage name can tell a wink, a journey or a public, but only the official source fixes the origin.",
]),
("The new wave: BONNA, Ras B and Litleboy", [
"<a href=\"/en/bonna.html\">BONNA</a> is the new talent of this roster: an artist from La Plaine, in Dominica, signed to More Sewo, and carried by the track \"Not Real\". His signing to a label and the circulation of his name in 2026 show how the Dominican next generation is getting structured: a village, a track that runs, a team behind it. BONNA is the name to watch for anyone who wants to hear where the source island's bouyon goes next, and his profile on this site follows that rise step by step.",
"<a href=\"/en/ras-b.html\">Ras B</a> works on the border of rap and bouyon, with the track \"Come Here\". That crossing matters: bouyon has always absorbed the sounds of its time, and rap brings it a flow, a writing style and a young public. <a href=\"/en/litleboy-lsbeats767.html\">Litleboy LSBeats767</a> completes this trio on the production and credits side: he is credited on \"Pow Pow / Allo Doudou\", the track certified gold by SNEP, and the 767 in his name is a reference to Dominica's area code. Even in a credit read from France, the source island stays signed in the name.",
]),
("The remix producer: Smokiller", [
"<a href=\"/en/smokiller.html\">Smokiller</a> closes this roster with a different profile: Dominican-born and now American, he is the producer behind the Bouyon Shattaz remixes, the ones that flip tracks from other worlds into bouyon. His place here is logical: the diaspora is part of the source island's story, and a Dominican-born producer active from the United States extends the sound of Roseau towards new publics. He is also the editorial signature of this site, published by Bouyon Shattaz.",
"Smokiller's work shows another way to defend the origin: through production. A successful bouyon remix imposes Dominica's rhythm and energy on songs the world already knows, then sends the listener back to the artists on this page.",
]),
("The home meeting point: WCMF 2026", [
"This roster meets on one stage: the 2026 World Creole Music Festival in Roseau, whose Night 1 bill includes Ridge, TK International and Colton T. Our page on the <a href=\"/en/wcmf-creole-festival.html\">WCMF, Dominica's Creole festival</a>, details the event. For the source island, this festival is not one more concert: it is the annual showcase where Dominica presents its music to the Creole world, in front of the public that watched it being born.",
"Preparing for WCMF with this roster changes the listening: every name read here becomes a set time not to miss. To extend the party, our <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a> gathers the tracks to know, from the founders to the new wave. This roster is not an archive: it is a living programme.",
]),
],
"faq": [
("Who created bouyon in Dominica?", "Bouyon was created by WCK in 1988 in Roseau, the capital of Dominica. That is the starting point of the whole genre and the first name of the Dominican roster presented on this page."),
("Why do Asa Bantan and Reo carry king and prince titles?", "Asa Bantan is widely called the King of Bouyon and Bouyon Boss, and Reo is billed as the Prince of Bouyon. These are titles attributed by the scene and the public, not official titles, and this site always presents them as such."),
("Which Dominican artists play Night 1 of WCMF 2026?", "Night 1 of the 2026 World Creole Music Festival includes Ridge, TK International, the Triple Kay Band, and Colton T. The festival details are on our dedicated WCMF page."),
],
"date_published": "2026-10-16",
},
